WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.474
Welcome. In this first video: the difference between a chat assistant and software that acts on its own, what one run looks like, and what employers ask for.

00:00:10.674 --> 00:00:16.305
Picture a support engineer at two in the morning typing one question: why did dock D1 reboot last night?

00:00:16.555 --> 00:00:20.096
Instead of guessing, the agent searches the device logs.

00:00:20.346 --> 00:00:38.446
Agent: An LLM in a loop that decides its own next step: it calls tools, reads the results, and keeps going until the goal is met or a limit is hit. Like: A technician with a toolbox and a ticket, not a script: they choose which tool to use next based on what they just saw.

00:00:38.746 --> 00:00:43.099
It checks which firmware the dock runs, and compares it with the known issues.

00:00:43.349 --> 00:00:53.566
Then it replies with the cause, the fix, and the exact log lines it relied on. Building that, safely and reliably, is the job this course prepares you for.

00:00:54.616 --> 00:00:56.845
Let's separate two words people mix up.

00:00:57.245 --> 00:01:04.083
A chatbot answers once, from what the model already learned. It is like asking a colleague a question across the desk.

00:01:04.483 --> 00:01:14.805
Model: A trained file of numbers plus the math that uses them to turn an input into an output. For text, the input is your words and the output is a guess at the next word. Like: A compiled lookup engine: firmware image (the numbers) plus the code that reads it.

00:01:15.055 --> 00:01:24.877
An agent is given a goal. It decides which tools to call, reads what comes back, and keeps going until the goal is met, like a technician working through a troubleshooting checklist.

00:01:25.277 --> 00:01:39.174
Tool: A function your program lets the model request: search, read a file, query a database, run a test. The model never runs it itself. Like: A button on a panel the model may ask the operator to press.

00:01:39.474 --> 00:01:45.511
And you, the engineer, build everything around the model: the loop, the tools, the limits and the checks.

00:01:46.711 --> 00:01:50.183
Here is one run of the agent from the story, step by step.

00:01:50.583 --> 00:01:52.591
A goal arrives from a user.

00:01:52.991 --> 00:01:58.262
The model reads it and picks the next step, for example: call the log search tool for D1.

00:01:58.662 --> 00:02:04.142
Your code checks that request and runs the tool. The model never touches your systems directly.

00:02:04.542 --> 00:02:06.841
The result goes back to the model as text.

00:02:07.241 --> 00:02:16.749
And the cycle repeats until the model gives a final answer, or one of your limits stops it. That cycle is called the agent loop, and you will write it yourself in lab five.

00:02:17.149 --> 00:02:34.831
Agent loop: The core cycle of every agent: call the model, if it asked for tools run them and append the results, call the model again, stop when it gives a final answer. Like: The main loop of firmware: read inputs, decide, act, repeat until shutdown.

00:02:35.931 --> 00:02:41.585
We read twelve current agentic engineer job postings. The same skills came up again and again.

00:02:41.985 --> 00:02:57.067
Agentic: Describes software where an LLM, not fixed code, chooses some of the control flow: which tool to call and when to stop. Like: A self-driving mode, as opposed to cruise control that only holds one setting.

00:02:57.367 --> 00:02:59.990
Python, and calling model APIs well.

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:08.297
API: Application programming interface: the set of calls one program offers another. Here, usually an HTTP endpoint you send JSON to. Like: The ioctl list a driver exposes: the only doors into it.

00:03:08.547 --> 00:03:11.844
Tool calling, and agent frameworks such as LangGraph.

00:03:12.244 --> 00:03:31.575
Tool calling (function calling): You describe functions to the model (name, purpose, parameters). Instead of answering in prose, the model can reply "call get_weather with city=Austin". Your code runs it and sends the result back. Like: The model writes a work order; your code is the technician who actually turns the screwdriver.

00:03:31.875 --> 00:03:48.210
Agent framework: A library that provides the agent loop, tool plumbing, state and tracing for you, so you write less glue code. Like: An RTOS instead of a hand-rolled super-loop: more features, more to learn, less control.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:04:06.761
LangGraph: A library (from the LangChain team) where you define an agent as a graph: nodes are steps, edges are transitions, a typed state object flows through, with checkpoints so runs can pause and resume. Like: Writing your agent as an explicit state machine with save points.

00:04:07.061 --> 00:04:12.088
RAG, which means answering from your company's own documents instead of the model's memory.

00:04:12.488 --> 00:04:29.241
RAG: Retrieval-augmented generation: before asking the model, search your own documents for the relevant passages and paste them into the prompt so it answers from them. Like: An open-book exam: first find the right pages, then write the answer from them.

00:04:29.541 --> 00:04:34.789
Evals and observability, which means measuring quality and seeing inside every run.

00:04:35.189 --> 00:04:53.684
Eval: A repeatable test for an LLM system: fixed inputs, an expected result or a grading rule, and a score. Because outputs vary, you measure pass rates rather than one pass/fail. Like: A production test fixture that measures yield across a batch, not one unit.

00:04:53.984 --> 00:05:09.320
Observability: Being able to answer "what happened and why?" from the outside, using traces, logs and metrics, without adding new code. Like: Having a debug UART, a trace port and counters wired out before the field failure, not after.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:15.135
And security plus production reliability. Each of these is a chapter, and each has a lab.

00:05:16.335 --> 00:05:20.445
Good news: you already know the hard parts, under different names.

00:05:20.845 --> 00:05:23.051
State machines become agent graphs.

00:05:23.451 --> 00:05:37.313
Graph (nodes and edges): A set of nodes (steps) connected by edges (allowed transitions). Conditional edges choose the next node from the current state. Like: A state-machine diagram: bubbles and arrows with guard conditions.

00:05:37.613 --> 00:05:40.597
Watchdog timers become step and cost limits.

00:05:40.997 --> 00:05:45.328
Logic analyser captures become traces of every model and tool call.

00:05:45.728 --> 00:06:04.153
Trace / span: A trace is the full record of one request through your system; it is made of spans, timed steps such as one model call or one tool call, with inputs, outputs, tokens and errors. Like: A logic analyser capture of one transaction, with each bus phase timestamped.

00:06:04.453 --> 00:06:11.093
And memory protection becomes tool permissions. Throughout the course, every new idea is tied back to one of these.

00:06:12.293 --> 00:06:18.075
Question for you: In one sentence: what makes the system in the story an agent rather than a chatbot?

00:06:24.375 --> 00:06:33.849
Answer: It decides on its own which tools to call, uses their results, and keeps going until the goal is met, instead of answering once from memory.

00:06:34.949 --> 00:06:35.599
Recap.

00:06:35.999 --> 00:06:38.878
An agent pursues a goal by calling tools in a loop.

00:06:39.278 --> 00:06:43.028
The model only suggests. Your code decides and executes.

00:06:43.428 --> 00:06:48.107
Employers want model APIs, tools, RAG, evals and safety.

00:06:48.507 --> 00:06:55.125
And your firmware instincts map directly onto all of it. Next: how a language model produces text at all.
